-- replay --
$ dorc explain --summary plan-lifecycle
A Dorc run has two halves separated by your approval. First, planning:
Dorc ships small read-only checks called probes to the host, all in
parallel, and folds what they measure into a plan - your book, in its
original order, with the already-done lines removed and a reason written
beside every line that stays. Nothing on any host has been changed yet.
Then, only after you accept it, the apply: the surviving lines run, in
book order, exactly as written. Probes measure, the plan proposes, the
apply acts; your consent sits in the middle, and it is the only
interaction there is.

$ dorc explain plan-lifecycle
plan-lifecycle

A Dorc run has two halves, and everything about how Dorc behaves follows
from which half you are in. The halves fail in opposite directions on
purpose: the first half, unsure, does less; the second half, unsure,
runs the line.

The first half is planning. Dorc reads your book, and for every command
an oracle describes, it lifts that oracle's own read-only check - a
probe - and ships the whole batch to the host at once, in parallel.
Probes only ever read: an oracle author has vouched, by writing the
check inside their oracle, that running it changes nothing. A command
whose oracle is unsure, or whose check looks like it could write, simply
does not get probed; the command stays in the plan and runs. Probing is
where Dorc's speed comes from, and it is also the half with the hardest
promise: presenting a plan is promising that nothing has happened yet.

What the probes measure folds into the plan. The plan is your book - the
same lines, the same order, nothing reordered or rewritten - with the
lines whose work is provably already done removed, and a reason written
beside every line that stays. Some lines stay because the world says
they are needed; some stay because nothing is known about them; and some
stay with a check placed in front of them, because their proof could not
be trusted all the way down the page:

   > $ dorc explain --terse wall
   > A wall is a command in your book that Dorc knows nothing about: no
   > fact measured above it can be trusted below it, so the commands
   > after a wall are re-checked or run instead of being removed from
   > the plan.

The plan is the moment of consent, and the only one. Dorc will never ask
you a question mid-run; every decision is in front of you, in the plan,
before anything acts.

The second half is the apply. The surviving lines run on the host, in
book order, exactly as written - Dorc never reorders your book, and
apply speed comes only from lines that are gone, never from lines run
cleverly. A guarded line re-checks itself in place and falls through to
running the original command if the check does not hold. If the world
turns out to differ from what the plan predicted, the apply proceeds and
flags the difference in its report; it does not stop to ask, because you
already consented to the plan, and the plan is the whole conversation.

see also: dorc explain wall; dorc explain oracle; dorc explain guard

